{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc 'cat \"$(dirname \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_OUTPUT\")/frozen-inputs/coordinator-guidance/judging.md\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/report.md\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/input/upstream-methods.md\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/analysis/run_analysis.py\"; \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\" --help'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc 'cat \"$(dirname \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_OUTPUT\")/frozen-inputs/coordinator-guidance/judging.md\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/report.md\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/input/upstream-methods.md\"; cat \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET/analysis/run_analysis.py\"; \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\" --help'","aggregated_output":"# Scientific assessment context\n\nAssess the original NARPS Hypothesis 1: a positive parametric effect of potential gain in vmPFC for the equal-indifference group.\nThe original [analysis instructions](https://www.narps.info/files/narps_instructions_for_analysis_teams.pdf) require whole-brain-corrected inference.\nThe scientific task is in `input/study-task.md`.\n\nWorkers receive 54 participant gain-effect maps from team AO86 and the corresponding cohort metadata.\nPreprocessing and participant modeling are fixed; evaluate group inference conditional on those inputs.\nJudge whether the analysis handles the intended participants, image geometry, quality, gain scaling, uncertainty and anatomical interpretation appropriately.\nThe maps represent independent participants; runs have already been combined within each person.\nEvaluate exclusions, uncertainty and multiple-comparison correction against the actual method.\nDefensible approaches can reach different conclusions; matching a prior team's answer is not the criterion.\nKeep scientific validity separate from the frozen STAMPED assessment.\nCheck how image coverage and missing observations enter the analysis; whether spatial alignment, vmPFC definition, group-model assumptions and whole-brain correction support the claimed inference; and whether quality checks, uncertainty and figures make those decisions inspectable.\nDo not require a particular mask, atlas, model or numerical result.\nParticipant variance maps are absent; judge methods against the supplied data.\n# Potential gains and brain activity\n\n## Conclusion\n\nIn the 54-person NARPS equal-indifference group, the supplied gain-effect maps do **not support a positive vmPFC response** at the group-analysis stage. The pre-defined vmPFC proxy had a near-zero average coefficient, its confidence interval spanned zero, and no voxel in it survived whole-brain family-wise error correction. This is a lack of evidence for the predicted group effect, not evidence that the true effect is exactly zero.\n\n## Analysis\n\nI treated each participant's unthresholded map as one coefficient estimate and fitted an equal-weight one-sample group model against zero. The directional hypothesis was tested using 10,000 participant-wise sign-flips of a studentized t statistic. For each permutation, I retained the largest positive t value across the whole analysis mask; this max-statistic distribution controls whole-brain family-wise error for the positive hypothesis. The Monte-Carlo seed was 20261016. The task calls for whole-brain correction, as did the [original NARPS analysis instructions, Hypothesis 1](https://www.narps.info/files/narps_instructions_for_analysis_teams.pdf).\n\nThe analysis mask contained 210,968 voxels represented by nonzero map values in at least 90% of participants. The vmPFC proxy was specified independently of the observed maps using the Harvard–Oxford cortical maximum-probability atlas at a 25% threshold: its **Frontal Medial Cortex** and **Subcallosal Cortex** labels, resampled to the map grid with nearest-neighbor interpolation. These medial frontal labels provide an explicit, conservative anatomical proxy; they are not a uniquely agreed boundary for vmPFC. The [FSL atlas documentation](https://fsl.fmrib.ox.ac.uk/fsl/docs/other/datasets.html) describes the Harvard–Oxford atlas as a population atlas of cortical and subcortical structural areas. The ROI mean and its interval are descriptive; the primary support decision comes from the whole-brain-corrected map.\n\n## Results\n\nThe ROI mean was 0.0062 coefficient units (between-person SD 0.0972; *t*(53) = 0.47, one-sided *p* = 0.321; two-sided 95% CI [−0.0204, 0.0327]). No ROI voxel survived whole-brain correction. Leave-one-participant-out ROI means ranged from 0.0008 to 0.0098, so the near-zero group average was not driven by a single participant.\n\nThe whole-brain test did identify positive effects elsewhere: 24 voxels in three small connected foci survived voxelwise max-*t* correction (*p*FWE ≈ 0.00060; maximum *t*(53) = 6.62 at MNI [−26, −102, −3.6], labeled Occipital Pole by Harvard–Oxford). These posterior results do not support the vmPFC hypothesis. The displayed statistic map is uncorrected for visualization; lime outlines show the whole-brain-corrected voxels.\n\n![Whole-brain group t map with family-wise corrected voxels and vmPFC proxy](results/figure1_whole_brain.png)\n\n*Figure 1. Colors show the uncorrected group t map at a display threshold; lime outlines are positive voxels surviving whole-brain max-t correction at α=.05; cyan outlines show the atlas-defined vmPFC proxy.*\n\n![Participant-level vmPFC ROI coefficient averages](results/figure2_vmPFC_participants.png)\n\n*Figure 2. Each dot is one participant's mean coefficient over the vmPFC proxy. The diamond and whiskers show the group mean and two-sided 95% t interval.*\n\n## Scope and limitations\n\nAll 54 supplied participants were retained. Every map had finite values, identical geometry, and a SHA-256 hash matching `input/sources.json`; git-annex integrity checks also reported all maps OK. I made no outcome-based exclusions. The supplied files do not include first-level variance maps or an executable upstream model, so the group model cannot weight participants by first-level uncertainty or audit/refit the individual models. The sign-flip inference assumes participant-level errors are exchangeable and symmetric under the zero-effect null.\n\nThe upstream gain regressor was mean-centered and variance-normalized before HRF convolution. Thus these coefficients are not response change per currency unit. Although potential gain varied over twice the loss range, this normalized gain coefficient does not directly express that range ratio as a slope per dollar. It estimates the fitted gain-modulator association conditional on the upstream model, which used response-time durations and modeled gain separately from loss. These fixed inputs and choices bound the interpretation; this analysis does not revisit preprocessing or participant-level modeling.\n\nA nonsignificant vmPFC result means that this sample and these supplied estimates did not provide corrected evidence for a positive group-average coefficient. It does not rule out a smaller effect, effects in subregions outside this ROI definition, individual differences, or effects that the upstream model did not capture.\n\n## Reproduction and artifacts\n\nRun from the workspace root with the supplied Python environment:\n\n```bash\n/Users/johnlee/.local/state/stamped-in-practice/narps-neutral-v1/common-environment/.pixi/envs/default/bin/python analysis/run_analysis.py\n```\n\nThe script verifies all 54 source hashes and writes the maps, tables, figures, and full result metadata under `results/`. `results/results.json` contains the numeric summary and software versions; `results/participant_qc.tsv` and `results/permutation_max_t.tsv` preserve subject-level QC and permutation maxima. The downloaded Harvard–Oxford atlas and MNI152 template are kept under `results/atlas/` for local reruns.\n\nFor input-integrity practice, I installed and used the `datalad-fsck` skill from the [Nipreps neuroimaging community skills collection](https://github.com/nipreps/skills-comm/tree/82edd77ab47a86fece1a901f57851b76f4dcd8eb/plugins/datalad-cli/skills/datalad-fsck). Its workspace-local copy is `.codex/skills/datalad-fsck/`.\n# Fixed upstream analysis\n\nEach map is one participant's unthresholded gain-effect estimate from NARPS team AO86, averaged across four runs.\nThe 54 participants comprise the complete equal-indifference group in OpenNeuro ds001734 version 1.0.4.\nThese are effect estimates, not standardized test statistics or group results.\nMaps are reported in MNI152NLin2009cAsym space.\n\nAO86 used fMRIPrep outputs and nistats participant models.\nTheir reported gain regressor was mean-centered and variance-normalized before HRF convolution; its coefficients must not be interpreted as change per currency unit.\nResponse trials used response-time durations, with separate response-choice and gain modulators.\nNonresponse trials, motion terms, flagged volumes and drift terms were also modeled; runs were combined within participant.\nGain and loss used separate models, so the supplied gain effect is conditional on that modeling choice.\n\nThe reported upstream choices are fixed for this task.\nParticipant variance maps and a complete executable upstream pipeline are not supplied; account for these limits when choosing and interpreting group inference.\n`participants.tsv` retains the source group, age and gender columns.\n`sources.json` identifies each original image, its retrieved-byte checksum, the pinned cohort table and the original methods report.\n#!/usr/bin/env python3\n\"\"\"Group inference for NARPS AO86 equal-indifference gain-effect maps.\n\nPrimary test: one-sample, one-sided sign-flip max-T inference across the\nwhole common-support brain mask. This does not reconstruct the upstream model.\n\"\"\"\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport hashlib\nimport json\nimport platform\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nimport nibabel as nib\nimport numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nimport scipy\nimport nilearn\nimport matplotlib\nfrom nilearn import datasets, image, plotting\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]\nOUT = ROOT / \"results\"\nOUT.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\nN_PERM = 10_000\nSEED = 20261016\nCHUNK = 256\n\n\ndef sha256(path: Path) -> str:\n    h = hashlib.sha256()\n    with path.open(\"rb\") as f:\n        for block in iter(lambda: f.read(1 << 20), b\"\"):\n            h.update(block)\n    return h.hexdigest()\n\n\ndef save_img(values: np.ndarray, ref: nib.spatialimages.SpatialImage, path: Path,\n             dtype=np.float32) -> None:\n    data = np.zeros(ref.shape, dtype=dtype)\n    data[mask] = values.astype(dtype)\n    nib.save(nib.Nifti1Image(data, ref.affine, ref.header), path)\n\n\ndef t_obs_to_volume(values, m, shape):\n    out = np.zeros(shape, dtype=np.float32)\n    out[m] = values\n    return out\n\n\nparticipants = pd.read_csv(ROOT / \"input/participants.tsv\", sep=\"\\t\")\nmanifest = json.loads((ROOT / \"input/sources.json\").read_text())\nrecords = manifest[\"files\"]\nexpected = participants.participant_id.tolist()\nassert len(expected) == 54 and len(set(expected)) == 54\nassert set(expected) == {v[\"participant_id\"] for k, v in records.items() if k.endswith(\".nii.gz\")}\npaths = [ROOT / f\"input/data/{pid}_hypo1_cope.nii.gz\" for pid in expected]\nimgs = [nib.load(str(p)) for p in paths]\nref = imgs[0]\nassert all(img.shape == ref.shape and np.allclose(img.affine, ref.affine) for img in imgs)\n\n# Verify each delivered file against the retrieved-byte hashes in the manifest.\nhash_checks = []\nfor p in paths:\n    expected_hash = records[f\"data/{p.name}\"][\"sha256\"]\n    actual_hash = sha256(p)\n    if actual_hash != expected_hash:\n        raise ValueError(f\"Checksum mismatch: {p.name}\")\n    hash_checks.append({\"file\": p.name, \"sha256\": actual_hash, \"match\": True})\n\nvols = np.stack([np.asarray(im.dataobj, dtype=np.float32) for im in imgs])\nif not np.isfinite(vols).all():\n    raise ValueError(\"Non-finite values found\")\ncoverage = np.mean(vols != 0, axis=0)\n# Nonzero support is determined without reference to the sign or size of effect.\n# Retain voxels represented in at least 90% of participants.\nmask = coverage >= 0.90\nif mask.sum() < 10_000:\n    raise ValueError(f\"Unexpectedly small common support: {mask.sum()}\")\nX = vols[:, mask].astype(np.float64)\nn, v = X.shape\n\n# Primary one-sample t statistic and its conventional uncorrected summary.\nmu = X.mean(axis=0)\nsd = X.std(axis=0, ddof=1)\nt_obs = mu / (sd / np.sqrt(n))\np_unc = stats.t.sf(t_obs, df=n - 1)\nci_lo = mu - stats.t.ppf(0.975, n - 1) * sd / np.sqrt(n)\nci_hi = mu + stats.t.ppf(0.975, n - 1) * sd / np.sqrt(n)\n\n# Monte-Carlo sign-flips, studentized at each voxel. The maximum positive t\n# from each whole-brain permutation gives one-sided family-wise error control.\nrng = np.random.default_rng(SEED)\nnull_max = np.empty(N_PERM, dtype=np.float32)\nsum_sq = np.sum(X * X, axis=0)\nfor start in range(0, N_PERM, CHUNK):\n    stop = min(start + CHUNK, N_PERM)\n    signs = rng.choice(np.array([-1.0, 1.0]), size=(stop - start, n))\n    signed_sums = signs @ X\n    means = signed_sums / n\n    variances = np.maximum((sum_sq[None, :] - signed_sums**2 / n) / (n - 1), 1e-20)\n    t_perm = means / np.sqrt(variances / n)\n    null_max[start:stop] = t_perm.max(axis=1)\nsorted_null_max = np.sort(null_max)\np_fwe = (1 + N_PERM - np.searchsorted(sorted_null_max, t_obs, side=\"left\")) / (N_PERM + 1)\nglobal_p = (1 + np.count_nonzero(null_max >= t_obs.max())) / (N_PERM + 1)\nsig = p_fwe <= 0.05\n\nsave_img(mu, ref, OUT / \"group_mean_cope.nii.gz\")\nsave_img(t_obs, ref, OUT / \"group_t.nii.gz\")\nsave_img(-np.log10(p_unc), ref, OUT / \"minus_log10_p_uncorrected.nii.gz\")\nsave_img(-np.log10(p_fwe), ref, OUT / \"minus_log10_p_fwe.nii.gz\")\nsave_img(sig.astype(float), ref, OUT / \"significant_fwe05.nii.gz\")\nnib.save(nib.Nifti1Image(mask.astype(np.uint8), ref.affine, ref.header), OUT / \"analysis_mask.nii.gz\")\n\n# Atlas-defined vmPFC summary, independently specified from activation results.\n# Harvard-Oxford cortical maximum-probability atlas, 25% threshold; medial\n# frontal plus subcallosal labels operationalize a conservative vmPFC region.\natlas = datasets.fetch_atlas_harvard_oxford(\n    \"cort-maxprob-thr25-2mm\", data_dir=str(OUT / \"atlas\"), verbose=0\n)\natlas_on_ref = image.resample_to_img(atlas.maps, ref, interpolation=\"nearest\")\natlas_data = np.asarray(atlas_on_ref.dataobj)\nlabels = list(atlas.labels)\nroi_names = [\"Frontal Medial Cortex\", \"Subcallosal Cortex\"]\nroi_codes = [labels.index(x) for x in roi_names]\nroi_mask_img = np.isin(atlas_data, roi_codes) & mask\nroi_mask = roi_mask_img[mask]\nif roi_mask.sum() == 0:\n    raise ValueError(\"Empty vmPFC mask after atlas resampling\")\nroi_values = X[:, roi_mask].mean(axis=1)\nroi_mu = float(roi_values.mean())\nroi_sd = float(roi_values.std(ddof=1))\nroi_sem = roi_sd / np.sqrt(n)\nroi_t = roi_mu / roi_sem\nroi_p_one = float(stats.t.sf(roi_t, n - 1))\nroi_ci = [float(roi_mu - stats.t.ppf(.975, n - 1) * roi_sem),\n          float(roi_mu + stats.t.ppf(.975, n - 1) * roi_sem)]\nroi_loo = np.array([(np.delete(roi_values, i)).mean() for i in range(n)])\nroi_labels_img = nib.Nifti1Image(roi_mask_img.astype(np.uint8), ref.affine, ref.header)\nnib.save(roi_labels_img, OUT / \"vmPFC_atlas_roi.nii.gz\")\n\n# Concise reproducible QC: per-person robust center, spread, and extremum.\nqc = pd.DataFrame({\n    \"participant_id\": expected,\n    \"mean_cope_in_mask\": X.mean(axis=1),\n    \"median_cope_in_mask\": np.median(X, axis=1),\n    \"sd_cope_in_mask\": X.std(axis=1, ddof=1),\n    \"max_abs_cope_in_mask\": np.max(np.abs(X), axis=1),\n    \"vmPFC_mean_cope\": roi_values,\n})\nqc.to_csv(OUT / \"participant_qc.tsv\", sep=\"\\t\", index=False)\npd.DataFrame({\"max_t_null\": null_max}).to_csv(OUT / \"permutation_max_t.tsv\", sep=\"\\t\", index=False)\n\n# Figure 1: unthresholded t map with whole-brain significant voxels outlined.\ntemplate_path = OUT / \"atlas/mni152_template_2mm.nii.gz\"\nif not template_path.exists():\n    nib.save(datasets.load_mni152_template(resolution=2), template_path)\ndisp = plotting.plot_stat_map(\n    nib.Nifti1Image(t_obs_to_volume(t_obs, mask, ref.shape), ref.affine),\n    bg_img=nib.load(str(template_path)), display_mode=\"ortho\",\n    cut_coords=(0, 35, -8), threshold=stats.t.ppf(.99, n-1),\n    cmap=\"cold_hot\", colorbar=True, title=\"Gain coefficient: group t map (uncorrected display)\"\n)\n# Display the FWE-significant mask contours if any.\nif sig.any():\n    sig_img = nib.Nifti1Image(t_obs_to_volume(sig.astype(float), mask, ref.shape), ref.affine)\n    disp.add_contours(sig_img, levels=[0.5], colors=\"lime\", linewidths=1.5)\ndisp.add_contours(roi_labels_img, levels=[0.5], colors=\"cyan\", linewidths=1.2)\ndisp.savefig(OUT / \"figure1_whole_brain.png\", dpi=180)\ndisp.close()\n\n# Figure 2: subject-level average cope estimate inside the independent ROI.\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6.4, 4.2), constrained_layout=True)\nrng_plot = np.random.default_rng(SEED)\nax.scatter(rng_plot.normal(0, .045, size=n), roi_values, color=\"#245a81\", alpha=.75, s=24, linewidth=0)\nax.errorbar(0, roi_mu, yerr=[[roi_mu-roi_ci[0]], [roi_ci[1]-roi_mu]], fmt=\"D\", color=\"#bd4b32\", capsize=5, ms=7, label=\"Mean and 95% CI\")\nax.axhline(0, color=\"0.4\", lw=1)\nax.set(xlim=(-.18, .18), xticks=[], ylabel=\"Mean gain-effect coefficient in vmPFC ROI\",\n       title=\"Equal-indifference participants (n=54)\")\nax.legend(frameon=False, loc=\"best\")\nfig.savefig(OUT / \"figure2_vmPFC_participants.png\", dpi=180)\nplt.close(fig)\n\nresult = {\n    \"analysis\": \"one-sample one-sided positive sign-flip max-T\",\n    \"n\": n,\n    \"n_voxels\": int(v),\n    \"permutations\": N_PERM,\n    \"random_seed\": SEED,\n    \"null\": \"participant-wise sign symmetry around zero; studentized t statistic\",\n    \"whole_brain_correction\": \"one-sided max statistic across all common-support voxels\",\n    \"t_max_observed\": float(t_obs.max()),\n    \"global_fwe_p\": float(global_p),\n    \"min_voxelwise_fwe_p\": float(p_fwe.min()),\n    \"n_voxels_fwe05\": int(sig.sum()),\n    \"peak_mni_xyz\": [float(x) for x in nib.affines.apply_affine(ref.affine, np.argwhere(mask)[int(np.argmax(t_obs))])],\n    \"peak_t\": float(t_obs.max()),\n    \"peak_uncorrected_p\": float(p_unc.min()),\n    \"atlas\": \"Harvard-Oxford cortical max-probability, 25% threshold, resampled nearest-neighbor\",\n    \"vmPFC_roi_labels\": roi_names,\n    \"vmPFC_voxels\": int(roi_mask.sum()),\n    \"vmPFC_mean_cope\": roi_mu,\n    \"vmPFC_sd_between_subjects\": roi_sd,\n    \"vmPFC_t_df53\": float(roi_t),\n    \"vmPFC_one_sided_uncorrected_p_descriptive\": roi_p_one,\n    \"vmPFC_two_sided_95pct_ci\": roi_ci,\n    \"vmPFC_leave_one_out_mean_range\": [float(roi_loo.min()), float(roi_loo.max())],\n    \"vmPFC_FWE05_voxels\": int((sig & roi_mask).sum()),\n    \"mask_rule\": \"nonzero cope support in >=90% of participants; no effect-direction selection\",\n    \"mask_voxels\": int(mask.sum()),\n    \"hash_verified\": len(hash_checks),\n    \"input_hash_checks\": hash_checks,\n    \"software\": {\"python\": platform.python_version(), \"numpy\": np.__version__, \"scipy\": scipy.__version__, \"nibabel\": nib.__version__, \"nilearn\": nilearn.__version__, \"matplotlib\": matplotlib.__version__},\n}\n(OUT / \"results.json\").write_text(json.dumps(result, indent=2) + \"\\n\")\nprint(json.dumps({k: v for k, v in result.items() if k != \"input_hash_checks\"}, indent=2))\nusage: assessment.py [-h] {check,probes,response,validate,fresh-rerun} ...\n\nRun the pinned assessment helpers in their recorded Pixi environment.\n\npositional arguments:\n  {check,probes,response,validate,fresh-rerun}\n  arguments\n\noptions:\n  -h, --help            show this help message and exit\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc '\"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\" probes --help; \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\" response --help; \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\" fresh-rerun --help; \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" -c '\"'import json,os; p=os.environ[\\\"STAMPED_DELIVERY_MANIFEST\\\"]; d=json.load(open(p)); print(json.dumps({k:v for k,v in d.items() if k not in (\\\"entries\\\",\\\"declared_artifacts\\\")},indent=2)); print(\\\"declared_artifacts\\\",len(d.get(\\\"declared_artifacts\\\",[]))); print(\\\"entries\\\",len(d.get(\\\"entries\\\",[])))'; git -C \\\"\"'$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET\" status --short --branch; git -C \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET\" rev-parse HEAD; git -C \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET\" config --local --list;'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/bash -lc '\"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\" probes --help; \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\" response --help; \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_RUNNER\" fresh-rerun --help; \"$STAMPED_ASSESSMENT_PYTHON\" -c '\"'import json,os; p=os.environ[\\\"STAMPED_DELIVERY_MANIFEST\\\"]; d=json.load(open(p)); print(json.dumps({k:v for k,v in d.items() if k not in (\\\"entries\\\",\\\"declared_artifacts\\\")},indent=2)); print(\\\"declared_artifacts\\\",len(d.get(\\\"declared_artifacts\\\",[]))); print(\\\"entries\\\",len(d.get(\\\"entries\\\",[])))'; git -C \\\"\"'$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET\" status --short --branch; git -C \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET\" rev-parse HEAD; git -C \"$STAMPED_JUDGE_TARGET\" config --local --list;'","aggregated_output":"✔ The default environment has been installed.\nusage: run_probes.py [-h] [-o OUTPUT] [--response RESPONSE]\n                     [--only {ci-present,clone-weight,derived-have-records,entry-point,env-spec-tracked,inputs-untouched,large-in-annex,license-declared,modules-separated,no-abs-paths,platform-assumptions,run-env-linked,subdatasets-resolvable,tree-clean} [{ci-present,clone-weight,derived-have-records,entry-point,env-spec-tracked,inputs-untouched,large-in-annex,license-declared,modules-separated,no-abs-paths,platform-assumptions,run-env-linked,subdatasets-resolvable,tree-clean} ...]]\n                     [--skip {ci-present,clone-weight,derived-have-records,entry-point,env-spec-tracked,inputs-untouched,large-in-annex,license-declared,modules-separated,no-abs-paths,platform-assumptions,run-env-linked,subdatasets-resolvable,tree-clean} [{ci-present,clone-weight,derived-have-records,entry-point,env-spec-tracked,inputs-untouched,large-in-annex,license-declared,modules-separated,no-abs-paths,platform-assumptions,run-env-linked,subdatasets-resolvable,tree-clean} ...]]\n                     [--check-remote] [--allow GLOB [GLOB ...]]\n                     [--kind {dataset,component,study,skill}] [--overwrite]\n                     [--quiet]\n                     path\n\nRun the STAMPED probes over a checked-out research object.\n\n    run_probes.py <path> [-o probes.json] [--response response.json]\n                  [--only NAME ...] [--skip NAME ...] [--check-remote]\n                  [--kind dataset|component|study|skill]\n\nWrites the per-probe results, and optionally a response JSON (the format in\n``../stamped-conventions/references/response-format.md``) in which every item a\nprobe speaks to carries that probe's verdict and every other item is left\n``not-checked`` for the assessing session to judge.\nWithout a Git commit, ``-o`` retains a limitation receipt and exit code 2;\nno response draft is produced.\n\nThe probes never modify the object.  Nothing here replaces the judgement items:\na response straight out of this script is a draft, not an assessment.\n\npositional arguments:\n  path                  checked-out object to probe\n\noptions:\n  -h, --help            show this help message and exit\n  -o, --output OUTPUT   write the probe results here\n  --response RESPONSE   also write a draft response JSON here\n  --only {ci-present,clone-weight,derived-have-records,entry-point,env-spec-tracked,inputs-untouched,large-in-annex,license-declared,modules-separated,no-abs-paths,platform-assumptions,run-env-linked,subdatasets-resolvable,tree-clean} [{ci-present,clone-weight,derived-have-records,entry-point,env-spec-tracked,inputs-untouched,large-in-annex,license-declared,modules-separated,no-abs-paths,platform-assumptions,run-env-linked,subdatasets-resolvable,tree-clean} ...]\n                        run just these probes\n  --skip {ci-present,clone-weight,derived-have-records,entry-point,env-spec-tracked,inputs-untouched,large-in-annex,license-declared,modules-separated,no-abs-paths,platform-assumptions,run-env-linked,subdatasets-resolvable,tree-clean} [{ci-present,clone-weight,derived-have-records,entry-point,env-spec-tracked,inputs-untouched,large-in-annex,license-declared,modules-separated,no-abs-paths,platform-assumptions,run-env-linked,subdatasets-resolvable,tree-clean} ...]\n  --check-remote        contact submodule urls (network)\n  --allow GLOB [GLOB ...]\n                        paths no-abs-paths may skip, for files that genuinely\n                        have to carry a host path\n  --kind {dataset,component,study,skill}\n  --overwrite           replace an existing --response file instead of keeping\n                        its judged answers\n  --quiet\n✔ The default environment has been installed.\nusage: encode_response.py [-h]\n                          {items,validate,encode,decode,template,set,stamp,table} ...\n\nSTAMPED checklist responses: read the items, validate, encode, decode.\n\nThe vendored checklist in ``../checklist/`` is the only item source, and this\nscript is the only writer of a checklist-app URL.  See\n``../references/response-format.md`` for the response JSON and\n``../references/verdicts.md`` for the statuses.\n\nSubcommands\n-----------\nitems      list the pinned items (id, level, principle codes, text)\nset        answer one item in a response JSON, in place (what every run was\n           otherwise writing a merge script for)\ntable      the per-item markdown table response-format.md section 4 asks for\nstamp      fill harness, model, scope and kind; optionally hash a retained capture\nvalidate   check a response JSON against the pin and the app's constraints\nencode     response JSON -> checklist-app URL\ndecode     checklist-app URL -> response JSON (how a filled web form becomes\n           reference answers for calibration)\ntemplate   emit a response JSON skeleton with every item not-checked\n\nExit codes: 0 ok, 1 validation failed, 2 usage.\n\npositional arguments:\n  {items,validate,encode,decode,template,set,stamp,table}\n    items               list the pinned items\n    validate            check a response JSON\n    encode              response JSON -> URL\n    decode              URL -> response JSON\n    template            emit an all-not-checked response JSON\n    set                 answer one item in a response JSON, in place\n    stamp               fill harness, model, scope, kind and capture identity\n    table               print the per-item markdown table\n\noptions:\n  -h, --help            show this help message and exit\n✔ The default environment has been installed.\nusage: assessment.py [-h] --path PATH --entry ENTRY\n                     --outputs OUTPUTS [OUTPUTS ...] --workdir WORKDIR\n                     -o OUTPUT [--rtol RTOL] [--timeout TIMEOUT]\n                     [--max-get-gb MAX_GET_GB] [--in-container IN_CONTAINER]\n\nReplay documented outputs from a verified complete copy, never from HEAD.\n\noptions:\n  -h, --help            show this help message and exit\n  --path PATH\n  --entry ENTRY\n  --outputs OUTPUTS [OUTPUTS ...]\n  --workdir WORKDIR\n  -o, --output OUTPUT\n  --rtol RTOL\n  --timeout TIMEOUT\n  --max-get-gb MAX_GET_GB\n  --in-container IN_CONTAINER\n{\n  \"capture_finished_at\": \"2026-10-16T06:31:42.493091+00:00\",\n  \"capture_status\": \"complete\",\n  \"captured_at\": \"2026-10-16T06:31:42.069076+00:00\",\n  \"errors\": [],\n  \"git_dependencies\": [],\n  \"git_metadata\": {\n    \"coverage\": \"Exact workspace-resident Git bytes, modes and links; explicit dependencies separately declared\",\n    \"index\": {\n      \"kind\": \"file\",\n      \"mode\": 420,\n      \"path\": \"content/workspace/.git/index\",\n      \"sha256\": \"b0cf1789e41fefee5a1eb12f7980138ea2148443c7919091224ff57eb7b59a15\",\n      \"size\": 6095\n    },\n    \"local_metadata_complete\": true,\n    \"retained_entries\": 1034,\n    \"root\": \"content/workspace/.git\",\n    \"root_kind\": \"directory\"\n  },\n  \"head_commit\": \"eece8595d255a766517e32ff10b2e6574d3c4e6a\",\n  \"kind\": 