ant effect maps, checks their recorded hashes, finite values and common geometry, performs an equal-weight one-sample directional group test, and controls positive whole-brain inference using a participant-wise sign-flip max-*t* distribution. Its vmPFC proxy is independently defined from Harvard\\u2013Oxford Frontal Medial and Subcallosal labels; the report distinguishes that proxy from a unique anatomical boundary. It reports effect scale limitations, missing first-level variance maps, uncertainty, and the distinction between no evidence and evidence of exact zero. The described ROI estimate is near zero (mean 0.0062, 95% CI \\u22120.0204 to 0.0327), with zero ROI voxels surviving the reported whole-brain correction. These methods and limits make the reported absence of corrected positive vmPFC evidence plausible and appropriately narrow.\",\n      \"start_line\": 15,\n      \"end_line\": 15\n    }\n  },\n  \"conclusion\": {\n    \"quote\": \"In the 54-person NARPS equal-indifference group, the supplied gain-effect maps do **not support a positive vmPFC response** at the group-analysis stage. The pre-defined vmPFC proxy had a near-zero average coefficient, its confidence interval spanned zero, and no voxel in it survived whole-brain family-wise error correction. This is a lack of evidence for the predicted group effect, not evidence that the true effect is exactly zero.\",\n    \"start_line\": 23,\n    \"end_line\": 23\n  }\n}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
